    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` rule for `POST /api/owners`, following the codebase's established pattern for derived owner attributes (identical to how `namesakeCount` is computed at create time, stored on the entity, and auto-mapped to the DTO). This guarantees the create response and any later `GET /api/owners/{id}` return a consistent value.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` (and every endpoint that returns owners) carries the field.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `bulkSignupWarning` (`Boolean`, column `bulk_signup_warning`) field with getter/setter, mirroring `namesakeCount`.\n\n3. **`FlagBulkSignup.java`** (new function class) \u2014 counts the owners already created for this owner's registration date (the same per-day accumulation `RequireDailyCapacity` uses) and sets `bulkSignupWarning = count > 80`. It runs before the owner is saved, so it counts existing owners \u2014 \"more than 80 have *already* been created today.\"\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `flagBulkSignup` step (transaction-governed) after `countNamesakes` and before `save`, so it runs after the registration date is applied and before persistence.\n\n5. **Schema files** (hsqldb/h2/postgres/mysql `schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN` column. The tests use HSQLDB, whose seed `data.sql` uses positional INSERTs, so I added a trailing `NULL` to each of the 10 owner rows to match the new column count. (MapStruct auto-maps the field \u2014 no mapper change needed.)\n\n**Verification:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. `AcceptanceTest.coreNoBulkWarningUnderThreshold` (create one owner, then GET expecting `bulkSignupWarning: false`) passes, and the >80 branch reuses the same counting path exercised by the daily-limit rule.\n\nOne design note worth surfacing: I interpreted \"created today\" as owners sharing this owner's effective registration date (the same key the existing daily-limit rule counts on), and evaluated the flag at create time excluding the owner being created \u2014 so the 82nd owner of a day is the first to be flagged. This matches both the spec wording (\"more than 80 owners have *already* been created\") and the test comment that the >80 branch \"shares the same accumulation path exercised by the daily-limit rule.\"",
